  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Down four from the start, they never found a swing that mattered.",
    "what_this_means": "The formula broke in the second inning when Detroit scored two, and it never healed. Jacob Lopez couldn't survive the sixth. Four runs crossed while he was still in the game. And by then the deficit had grown too wide for a lineup that struck out twelve times and scored once on a runner already in motion. Ginn held the damage to two earned runs across four innings, which was good enough to win most nights. This wasn't most nights. Seven hits, one run. That's not a close game pretending to be close. That's a team that had the pieces but not the order. The bats showed up just enough to remind you they could have done something, then disappeared. Inconsistency isn't a slump. It's a choice made five times a week.",
    "what_to_watch": "Detroit's bullpen exposed what's been sitting underneath this whole streak: when the Athletics need to string together quality at-bats, they scatter instead. Leave them trailing early and they chase with their eyes closed, chasing strikeouts instead of contact. Four straight losses now, and the pattern holds. Good enough pitching, not enough anything else. Twelve strikeouts and one run is a recipe no team survives twice.",
